{:edb/db-before-t 1682 :edb/db-after-t 1683 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534995 50 #inst "2026-10-08T13:42:22.557Z" 13194139534995 true] [17592186228085 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325519 13194139534995 true] [17592186228085 1104 #edb/ref 17592186325519 13194139534995 true] [17592186228859 1100 "Given that $\\displaystyle J(t)=\\int_{0}^{1} (\\cos t+2x)^2\\, \\textrm{d}x$, calculate $J'(t)$ for $t\\in\\mathbb R$ by differentiating under the integral sign." 13194139534995 false] [17592186228859 1100 "Given that $\\displaystyle J(t)=\\int_{0}^{1} (\\cos t+2x)^2\\, \\textrm{d}x,$ calculate $J'(t)$ for $t\\in\\mathbb R$ by differentiating under the integral sign." 13194139534995 true] [17592186228859 1145 "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on\n\n$$\nR = [a,b]\\times [c,d]\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}\n$$\n\nexists and is continuous on $R$. Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t) = \\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d)$, and\n\n$$\nF'(t) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t}\\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x = \\int_{a}^b \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x\n$$\n\nThis is known as differentiating under the integral sign.\n\nWe wish to compute $J'(t)$, where\n\n$$\nJ(t)=\\int_{0}^{1} (\\cos t+2x)^2\\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\nJ'(t) = \\int_{0}^{1} \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial t}\\Bigl[(\\cos t+2x)^2\\Bigr]\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(\\cos t + 2x)^{2}] &= 2(\\cos t + 2x) \\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(\\cos t + 2x) \\\\\n&= 2(\\cos t + 2x) \\cdot (-\\sin t) \\\\\n&=-2(\\cos t + 2x)\\sin t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nJ^{′}(t) &= ∫_{0}^{1} - 2(\\cos t + 2x)\\sin tdx \\\\\n&=-2\\sin t∫_{0}^{1}(\\cos t + 2x)dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{1}(\\cos t + 2x)dx &= \\cos t \\cdot x \\mid _{0}^{1} + x^{2} \\mid _{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\cos t + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nJ'(t) =-2\\sin t\\,(\\cos t+1)\n$$" 13194139534995 false] [17592186228859 1145 "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R = [a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t) = \\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\nF'(t) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t}\\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x\n=\n\\int_{a}^b \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as differentiating under the integral sign.\n\nWe wish to compute $J'(t),$ where\n\n$$\nJ(t)=\\int_{0}^{1} (\\cos t+2x)^2\\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\nJ'(t) = \\int_{0}^{1} \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial t}\\Bigl[(\\cos t+2x)^2\\Bigr]\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2x)^{2}] & =2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2x)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2x) \\\\  & =2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2x)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =-2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot x{|}_{0}^{1}+{x}^{2}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nJ'(t) =-2\\sin t\\,(\\cos t+1).\n$$" 13194139534995 true] [17592186228874 1100 "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n- $\\displaystyle F_1(t) = \\int_0^1 t\\cos x\\,\\textrm{d} x, \\quad t \\in \\mathbb R$\n\n- $\\displaystyle F_2(t) = \\int_0^1 \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{|x - t|}}\\,\\textrm{d} x, \\quad t \\in (0,1)$\n\n- $\\displaystyle F_3(t) = \\int_0^1 \\dfrac{1}{1 + (x - t)^2}\\,\\textrm{d} x, \\quad t \\in [0,1]$" 13194139534995 true] [17592186228874 1100 "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n1. $\\displaystyle F_1(t) = \\int_0^1 t\\cos x\\,\\textrm{d} x, \\quad t \\in \\mathbb R$\n2. $\\displaystyle F_2(t) = \\int_0^1 \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{\\mid x - t \\mid}}\\,\\textrm{d} x, \\quad t \\in (0,1)$\n3. $\\displaystyle F_3(t) = \\int_0^1 \\dfrac{1}{1 + (x - t)^2}\\,\\textrm{d} x, \\quad t \\in [0,1]$" 13194139534995 false] [17592186228874 1145 "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on\n\n$$\nR = [a,b]\\times [c,d]\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}\n$$\n\nexists and is continuous on $R$. Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t) = \\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d)$, and\n\n$$\nF'(t) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t}\\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x = \\int_{a}^b \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have\n$f_1(x,t) = t\\cos x$.\nWe fix $t_0 \\in \\mathbb R$ and choose $c < t_0 < d$ such that $[c,d] \\subseteq \\mathbb R$, so $R = [0,1] \\times [c,d]$.Note that $f_1$ is continuous on $R$. Computing the partial derivative, we have\n$\\dfrac{\\partial f_1}{\\partial t} = \\cos x$\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R$. Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at $t_0$. And since our choice of $t_0$ was arbitrary, we can compute $F'_1(t)$ for all real $t$.\n- For the second function, we have\n$f_2(x,t) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{\\mid x - t \\mid}}$.\nWe fix $t_0 \\in (0,1)$ and choose $c < t_0 < d$ such that $[c,d] \\subseteq (0,1)$, so $R = [0,1] \\times [c,d]$.Note that $f_2$ is undefined when $x = t$, since the denominator vanishes. Thus, $f_2$ is not continuous on $R$, and differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n- For the third function, we have\n$f_3(x,t) = \\dfrac{1}{1 + (x - t)^2}$.\nWe fix $t_0 \\in (0,1)$ and choose $c < t_0 < d$ such that $[c,d] \\subseteq [0,1]$, so $R = [0,1] \\times [c,d]$.Note that $f_3$ is continuous on $R$. Computing the partial derivative, we have\n$\\dfrac{\\partial f_3}{\\partial t} = \\dfrac{2(x - t)}{(1 + (x - t)^2)^2}$\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R$. Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at $t_0$. And since our choice of $t_0$ was arbitrary, we can compute $F'_3(t)$ for all $t \\in [0,1]$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534995 false] [17592186228874 1145 "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R = [a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t) = \\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\nF'(t) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t}\\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x\n=\n\\int_{a}^b \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\nf_1(x,t) = t\\cos x.\n$$\n\nWe fix $t_0 \\in \\mathbb R$ and choose $c < t_0 < d$ such that $[c,d] \\subseteq \\mathbb R,$ so $R = [0,1] \\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that $f_1$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f_1}{\\partial t} = \\cos x\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at $t_0.$ And since our choice of $t_0$ was arbitrary, we can compute $F'_1(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\nf_2(x,t) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{|x - t|}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix $t_0 \\in (0,1)$ and choose $c < t_0 < d$ such that $[c,d] \\subseteq (0,1),$ so $R = [0,1] \\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that $f_2$ is undefined when $x = t,$ since the denominator vanishes. Thus, $f_2$ is not continuous on $R,$ and differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\nf_3(x,t) = \\dfrac{1}{1 + (x - t)^2}.\n$$\n\nWe fix $t_0 \\in (0,1)$ and choose $c < t_0 < d$ such that $[c,d] \\subseteq [0,1],$ so $R = [0,1] \\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that $f_3$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f_3}{\\partial t} = \\dfrac{2(x - t)}{(1 + (x - t)^2)^2}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at $t_0.$ And since our choice of $t_0$ was arbitrary, we can compute $F'_3(t)$ for all $t \\in [0,1].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534995 true] [17592186229140 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325520 13194139534995 true] [17592186229140 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325523 13194139534995 true] [17592186229140 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325562 13194139534995 true] [17592186229140 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325565 13194139534995 true] [17592186229141 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325527 13194139534995 true] [17592186229141 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325534 13194139534995 true] [17592186229141 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325541 13194139534995 true] [17592186229141 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325548 13194139534995 true] [17592186229141 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325555 13194139534995 true] [17592186229605 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534995 true] [17592186229605 1100 "Given that $I(t) = ∫_{0}^{1}(2x + t^{3})^{2}dx$, calculate $I^{′}(t)$ for $t ∈ R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.$I^{′}(t) =$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534995 false] [17592186229605 1100 "Given that $I(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{t}^{3})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534995 true] [17592186229605 1145 "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${I}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nI(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{t}^{3})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(2x+{t}^{3})^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(2x+{t}^{3})^{2}] & =2(2x+{t}^{3})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(2x+{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =2(2x+{t}^{3})\\cdot 3{t}^{2} \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}(2x+{t}^{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6{t}^{2}(2x+{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{|}_{0}^{1}+{t}^{3}x{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1+{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2}(1+{t}^{3}).\n$$" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325519 1034 "6t^2(1+t^3)" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325519 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534995 true] [17592186325519 1111 #uuid "c56f45b8-10dd-51a2-b414-43379bcbec60" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325520 1017 #edb/ref 17592186325521 13194139534995 true] [17592186325520 1089 "q-334931" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325520 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534995 true] [17592186325520 1100 "Given that $I(t)={\\int }_{0}^{2}(x-4{t}^{3})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325520 1113 #uuid "62383116-90e1-54f6-a3e6-be20ccf80a44" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325520 1145 "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${I}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nI(t)={\\int }_{0}^{2}(x-4{t}^{3})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(x-4{t}^{3})^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(x-4{t}^{3})^{2}] & =2(x-4{t}^{3})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(x-4{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =2(x-4{t}^{3})\\cdot (-12{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =-24{t}^{2}(x-4{t}^{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}-24{t}^{2}(x-4{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-24{t}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x-4{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x-4{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{2}4{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{0}^{2}-4{t}^{3}x{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2-8{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & =-24{t}^{2}(2-8{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =48{t}^{2}(4{t}^{3}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325521 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325522 13194139534995 true] [17592186325521 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534995 true] [17592186325521 1041 "field-1" 13194139534995 true] [