ting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{-2}^{3}-2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t){\\int }_{-2}^{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-2x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-2}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{-2}^{3}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\,x{|}_{-2}^{3}-{x}^{2}{|}_{-2}^{3} \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{′}(t) & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\cdot (5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-5) \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325524 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325525 13194139534995 true] [17592186325524 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325526 13194139534995 true] [17592186325524 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534995 true] [17592186325524 1041 "field-1" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325524 1104 #edb/ref 17592186325526 13194139534995 true] [17592186325524 1110 #uuid "ba15556f-19c5-54ff-a8cc-a19c1dd83e84" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325525 1034 "1+\\cos(t)" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325525 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534995 true] [17592186325525 1111 #uuid "44b92202-2503-5b5d-be00-fba7f4e2f445" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325526 1034 "1-\\cos(t)" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325526 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534995 true] [17592186325526 1111 #uuid "a291c3d1-ccb8-5c81-abe2-1cc060ebdc5c" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325527 1017 #edb/ref 17592186325528 13194139534995 true] [17592186325527 1089 "q-335246" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325527 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534995 true] [17592186325527 1100 "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n- ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{|x-t|}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$\n- ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x}{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n- ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+x+t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325527 1113 #uuid "11579158-b44f-5ea4-9087-c8b32428e69f" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325527 1145 "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{|x-t|}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is undefined when $x=t,$ since the denominator vanishes. Thus, ${f}_{1}$ is not continuous on $R,$ and differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{x}{1+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=-\\frac{2tx}{(1+{t}^{2})^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{1+x+t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊂(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=-\\frac{1}{(1+x+t)^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t\\in (0,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325528 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325529 13194139534995 true] [17592186325528 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325530 13194139534995 true] [17592186325528 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325531 13194139534995 true] [17592186325528 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325532 13194139534995 true] [17592186325528 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325533 13194139534995 true] [17592186325528 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534995 true] [17592186325528 1041 "selection" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325528 1104 #edb/ref 17592186325530 13194139534995 true] [17592186325528 1110 #uuid "1156a224-821a-58c6-98e0-870accccf934" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325529 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325529 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534995 true] [17592186325529 1111 #uuid "ce29aa53-6f74-5d83-ab7a-5ab1a5ecd6d5" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325530 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325530 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534995 true] [17592186325530 1111 #uuid "de5f6e3f-f160-5f20-b543-82cdf56636d6" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325531 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325531 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534995 true] [17592186325531 1111 #uuid "5ab3f82a-594c-5659-bdbf-7d2a4ee4e216" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325532 1034 "None" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325532 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534995 true] [17592186325532 1111 #uuid "5ccd1448-2d96-5b02-9851-81802a829220" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325533 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325533 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534995 true] [17592186325533 1111 #uuid "b59a4e00-f2a6-5893-8b15-a916a3e4c062