e partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=\\frac{x({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})-2x{t}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t>0.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{x-t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>1$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(1,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that for $t>1$ and $x\\in [0,1],$ the denominator $x-t$ never vanishes on $R,$ so ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t>1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325535 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325536 13194139534995 true] [17592186325535 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325537 13194139534995 true] [17592186325535 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325538 13194139534995 true] [17592186325535 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325539 13194139534995 true] [17592186325535 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325540 13194139534995 true] [17592186325535 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534995 true] [17592186325535 1041 "selection" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325535 1104 #edb/ref 17592186325536 13194139534995 true] [17592186325535 1110 #uuid "4f539163-fa77-5396-898b-2e48d80e3e10" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325536 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325536 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534995 true] [17592186325536 1111 #uuid "6f5629e6-d958-5a8e-8c2b-b77a15f25f1f" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325537 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325537 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534995 true] [17592186325537 1111 #uuid "72ef