4139534995 true] [17592186325555 1113 #uuid "ea250752-4cd0-5e27-8e08-643028b0da10" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325555 1145 "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=|x-t|.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ However, the partial derivative\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}={\\begin{aligned}-1,\\,x>t \\\\ 1,\\,x<t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ndoes *not* exist whenever $x=t={t}_{0},$ and is therefore not defined everywhere on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{t}{1+{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=\\frac{(1+{x}^{2}+{t}^{2})-2{t}^{2}}{(1+{x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}}=\\frac{1+{x}^{2}-{t}^{2}}{(1+{x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{(x+t+1)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=-\\frac{3}{(x+t+1)^{4}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t\\in (0,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325556 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325557 13194139534995 true] [17592186325556 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325558 13194139534995 true] [17592186325556 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325559 13194139534995 true] [17592186325556 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325560 13194139534995 true] [17592186325556 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325561 13194139534995 true] [17592186325556 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534995 true] [17592186325556 1041 "selection" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325556 1104 #edb/ref 17592186325558 13194139534995 true] [17592186325556 1110 #uuid "ce9473a1-88e9-535f-ae1a-c4793a20bedb" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325557 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325557 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534995 true] [17592186325557 1111 #uuid "8785fc80-c16a-5f0a-aa6b-1aa3abf9e635" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325558 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325558 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534995 true] [17592186325558 1111 #uuid "dc2e51d1-2c12-59d4-a990-faed7dcb3bc5" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325559 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325559 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534995 true] [17592186325559 1111 #uuid "4388d650-bb8a-528f-83b1-0c2c13058ffb" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325560 1034 "None" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325560 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534995 true] [17592186325560 1111 #uuid "a291cc29-6539-5e0e-a273-38f95629afc1" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325561 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325561 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534995 true] [17592186325561 1111 #uuid "28f2af23-51cf-5201-b2a6-5cab51067938" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325562 1017 #edb/ref 17592186325563 13194139534995 true] [17592186325562 1089 "q-350549" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325562 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534995 true] [17592186325562 1100 "Given that $I(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3{x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325562 1113 #uuid "259fa305-7a07-58cd-bad6-735f377f064d" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325562 1145 "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${I}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nI(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3{x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(3{x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(3{x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}] & =2(3{x}^{2}+{t}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(3{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =2(3{x}^{2}+{t}^{2})\\cdot 2t \\\\  & =4t(3{x}^{2}+{t}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4t(3{x}^{2}+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4t{\\int }_{0}^{1}(3{x}^{2}+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(3{x}^{2}+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{3}{|}_{0}^{1}+{t}^{2}x{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1+{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)=4t(1+{t}^{2}).\n$$" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325563 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325564 13194139534995 true] [17592186325563 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534995 true] [17592186325563 1041 "field-1" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325563 1104 #edb/ref 17592186325564 13194139534995 true] [17592186325563 1110 #uuid "09e5b403-6604-5960-8130-feded318602a" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325564 1034 "4t(1+t^2)" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325564 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534995 true] [17592186325564 1111 #uuid "22fee74e-9c12-5e28-9bdf-f4a0eea9568b" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325565 1017 #edb/ref 17592186325566 13194139534995 true] [17592186325565 1089 "q-352879" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325565 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534995 true] [17592186325565 1100 "Given that $I(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}-{e}^{-t})^{3}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=3{e}^{-t}(\\,$\n{{field-1}}$)$" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325565 1113 #uuid "13045f32-cece-5ee0-ad5f-5c153a7a3a4a" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325565 1145 "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${I}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nI(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}-{e}^{-t})^{3}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[({x}^{2}-{e}^{-t})^{3}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[({x}^{2}-{e}^{-t})^{3}] & =3({x}^{2}-{e}^{-t})^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}({x}^{2}-{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =3({x}^{2}-{e}^{-t})^{2}\\cdot {e}^{-t} \\\\  & =3{e}^{-t}({x}^{2}-{e}^{-t})^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{e}^{-t}({x}^{2}-{e}^{-t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{e}^{-t}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}-{e}^{-t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}-{e}^{-t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}{e}^{-t}+{e}^{-2t})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{1}2{x}^{2}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-2t}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}{|}_{0}^{1}-{e}^{-t}\\cdot \\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}{|}_{0}^{1}+{e}^{-2t}\\cdot x{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{-t}+{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)=3{e}^{-t}(\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{-t}+{e}^{-2t}).\n$$" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325566 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325567 13194139534995 true] [17592186325566 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325568 13194139534995 true] [17592186325566 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534995 true] [17592186325566 1041 "field-1" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325566 1104 #edb/ref 17592186325567 13194139534995 true] [17592186325566 1110 #uuid "2ada744c-41c1-5a29-8b0c-17d0fdb4d4bc" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325567 1034 "\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{2}{3}e^{-t}+e^{-2t}" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325567 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534995 true] [17592186325567 1111 #uuid "4c71fb3b-f2a5-5244-a635-82a4a8ba4e51" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325568 1034 "\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{2}{3}e^{-t}+e^{-2t}" 13194139534995 true] [17592186325568 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534995 true] [17592186325568 1111 #uuid "dd6f9b8c-e5cb-5598-97d1-01af89ed124e" 13194139534995 true]] :edb/tempids {"q-332944/field-1/982fc2a253b2a13d824573e57ad976d43065eb9f2f7e4e980b83042f1988faf5" 17592186325519 "q-334931" 17592186325520 "q-334931/field-1" 17592186325521 "q-33493